markdown <<MARKDOWN
# TAG Protocol for Meaningful Feedback

When teams are ready for feedback (typically around Day 8 / Milestone 4), use the TAG protocol to guide structured, meaningful peer feedback.


TAG helps students move beyond surface-level comments and engage in feedback that improves both the product and their thinking.

## How TAG Works

<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; max-width: 900px; margin: auto;">

  <!-- TELL -->
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/b7b707ce75276f775ec583954ec65bf4-image-1776956980531.png" alt="icon for stength with fist" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px;">
    <div style="flex: 1; background: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
      <p style="font-size: 14pt;"><b>1. Tell: Identify Strengths</b></p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">
        Students highlight something that is working well in the app.
        <br><br>
        This reinforces strong ideas and helps teams recognize what to keep.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;"><i><b>Goal:</b> Build confidence and clarity around what is already effective.</i></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ASK -->
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/6b33a4aff94907563a6ed75218fae485-image-1776957078956.png" alt="Icon with quesiton marks" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px;">
    <div style="flex: 1; background: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
      <p style="font-size: 14pt;"><b>2. Ask: Clarify Understanding</b></p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">
        Students ask questions about how the app works or why decisions were made.
        <br><br>
        This helps uncover confusion or gaps in communication.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;"><i><b>Goal:</b> Surface misunderstandings and prompt deeper thinking.</i></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- GIVE -->
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/948548ff076e40fbdaec850ae53eb3e9-image-1776957143962.png" alt="lightbulb with suggestions icon" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto; border-radius: 10px;">
    <div style="flex: 1; background: #F5F7F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
      <p style="font-size: 14pt;"><b>3. Give: Suggest Improvements</b></p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;">
        Students offer a concrete suggestion to improve the app.
        <br><br>
        Suggestions should be specific and actionable.
      </p>
      <p style="font-size: 12pt;"><i><b>Goal:</b> Move the project forward with clear next steps.</i></p>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

---

<h2>Keep TAG Flexible</h2>

<p>
Students do <strong>not</strong> need a finished app to use TAG. Once a team has
<strong>one working interaction</strong>, they can get useful feedback and make better decisions before building too far in one direction.
</p>

  <p style="font-size:12pt; margin:0;">
    TAG can happen in quick, flexible ways: with a neighboring group, during a teacher spot-check, or when students review AI-generated feedback.
  </p>

<br>
<p>
The goal is consistent, meaningful feedback, not a rigid format.
</p>


<div style="background-color: #e4e2f8; border-radius: 10px; color: #000000; width: 95%; padding: 1px 20px;">

<p style="line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px; font-size: 12pt;">
<b>💡 Teacher Tip:</b> Remind students that good designers make thoughtful choices about feedback. They might <b>use</b> a suggestion, <b>save it for later</b>, or <b>reject it</b> if it doesn't support their design goals. Ask them to explain their reasoning in the Project Guide.
</p>

</div>


MARKDOWN
